# Design: Replay-safe Steel session checkpoints

## Problem
Inngest persists step outputs, so checkpointing the public connection handle exposed the long-lived Steel API key. Replays could also resume with a different API key or base URL and release against the wrong configuration.

## Approach
Persist API-key-free session metadata plus a one-way fingerprint of the normalized Steel endpoint and credential. Rebuild the public connection URL in memory using URL query encoding, and reject configuration drift before handler execution or release.

## Key Trade-offs
- The API key, base URL, and complete connectUrl never enter step state.
- Credential or endpoint rotation during an active run now fails explicitly and relies on the finite session timeout instead of risking a wrong-target release.
- The SDK-provided websocket URL remains checkpointed because it is required to reconnect without replay-time resource access after release.

## Verification
81 tests, TypeScript typecheck, ESM/CJS build, packed Bundler and Node16 consumers, runtime import/require, and production audit all pass. The live Steel E2E remains an explicit billable manual gate.
